# Compose environment for the Azmoth stack.
#
#     cp infra/docker/.env.example infra/docker/.env
#
# Compose reads `.env` from the directory holding the compose file, which is this one — so it is
# picked up automatically by both:
#
#     docker compose -f infra/docker/docker-compose.yml     up --build   # production, standalone
#     docker compose -f infra/docker/docker-compose.dev.yml up --build   # development
#
# Every value below has a working default in the compose files; this file exists to document what
# there is to set and to hold the values a real deployment must override. `.env` itself is
# gitignored — nothing in this repository should ever contain a production credential.
#
# ── This file describes TWO different shapes, and they no longer overlap much ──────────────────
# The LOCAL stack (docker-compose.yml / .dev.yml) runs everything on one machine: a `postgres`
# container, a `marketing` container, `adminer`, published ports. That is the right thing for a
# laptop and it is unchanged.
#
# The AZURE deployment (docker-compose.yml + docker-compose.azure.yml) runs three services and
# nothing else — caddy, engine, web. Postgres is Neon's, external. The marketing site is Vercel's.
# So the POSTGRES_* and MARKETING_* values below are LOCAL-ONLY, and the Azure deployment needs two
# Neon connection strings and a registry token instead.
#
# On the VM none of this is edited by hand: scripts/deploy.sh writes /opt/azmoth/shared/.env and
# leaves it alone on every deploy after the first. See docs/deploy/RUNBOOK.md.


# ══ THE AZURE DEPLOYMENT ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── The database: Neon, external ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# TWO connection strings, and they are NOT interchangeable. Both come out of the Neon console's
# Connect dialog; the difference is the "Connection pooling" toggle, and it shows up in the
# hostname as a `-pooler` infix.
#
#   DATABASE_URL         ep-cool-darkness-a1b2c3d4.eu-central-1.aws.neon.tech
#   DATABASE_URL_POOLED  ep-cool-darkness-a1b2c3d4-pooler.eu-central-1.aws.neon.tech
#                                                 ^^^^^^^
#
# ── DATABASE_URL — the DIRECT (non-pooled) endpoint ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Used by four things:
#
#   * `alembic upgrade head`               (the engine-migrate one-shot, on every deploy)
#   * Better Auth's table creation         (the web-auth-migrate one-shot)
#   * the engine at runtime                (the engine service)
#   * `pg_dump`                            (infra/scripts/backup-to-azure.sh)
#
# The first two are migrations, and Neon's own guidance is explicit: "using a pooled connection
# string for migrations can be prone to errors. For this reason, we recommend using a direct
# (non-pooled) connection when performing migrations." A transaction-mode pooler does not support
# session-level advisory locks (how a migration tool stops two runners colliding), SQL-level
# PREPARE/DEALLOCATE, or CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY. The fourth needs one consistent snapshot
# across hundreds of statements, which is the same problem.
#
# The third — the engine at RUNTIME — is the surprising one, so it is worth the paragraph.
#
# Neon's pooler is PgBouncer in transaction mode. SQLAlchemy's asyncpg dialect calls
# `asyncpg.connection.prepare()` for EVERY statement, and asyncpg names those statements
# `__asyncpg_stmt_N__` from a per-connection counter. Through a transaction-mode pooler that is the
# classic source of intermittent `DuplicatePreparedStatementError`.
#
# `?prepared_statement_cache_size=0` is the widely-repeated fix and it does not work: it clears
# SQLAlchemy's reuse cache, but both code paths still call `prepare(..., name=...)` with a default
# name function returning None, so asyncpg still mints the numbered name. `?statement_cache_size=0`
# is worse — SQLAlchemy passes it through as a string and asyncpg raises TypeError at startup.
# The configuration SQLAlchemy actually prescribes is `prepared_statement_name_func` plus
# `NullPool`, and both are Python arguments to `create_async_engine` in
# apps/engine/app/db/session.py — not expressible in a URL.
#
# The engine does not need a pooler anyway: it is ONE long-lived container holding at most fifteen
# connections (pool_size=5, max_overflow=10).
#
# NOTE the `+asyncpg` suffix. It is SQLAlchemy's — the driver is part of the URL. Neon gives you a
# plain `postgresql://`; add `+asyncpg` for the engine. apps/web/lib/auth-db.ts strips it and
# `pg_dump` is handed a stripped copy, so one value with the suffix serves all three consumers.
#
# ** QUOTE IT. ** Neon's string ends in a query string — typically
# '?sslmode=require&channel_binding=require' — and an unquoted '&' in a file that a shell sources is
# a background operator. This file's deployed counterpart IS sourced by a shell, in three places
# (infra/scripts/backup-to-azure.sh, deploy.sh's registry login, and `make azure-psql`), and the
# parse error abandons every line AFTER the offending one. So the symptom is not a broken database
# URL — it is the backup job reporting that STORAGE_ACCOUNT is unset. Compose strips the quotes.
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql+asyncpg://azmoth:PASSWORD@ep-cool-darkness-a1b2c3d4.eu-central-1.aws.neon.tech/azmoth?sslmode=require&channel_binding=require"

# ── DATABASE_URL_POOLED — the POOLED endpoint ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used by exactly one thing: Better Auth at runtime, in the `web` service.
#
# Safe there in a way it is not in the engine, because Better Auth talks to Postgres through
# node-postgres (`pg`), which issues plain unnamed queries and does not use protocol-level prepared
# statements. And beneficial there: every Next.js route handler is a short burst of queries with
# idle gaps, which is exactly the connection-per-request shape the pooler exists for. On the direct
# endpoint a busy moment could open one connection per concurrent request.
#
# If it is unset, the web tier falls back to DATABASE_URL. That is correct, just chattier — which
# is why its absence is easy to miss and why scripts/deploy.sh backfills it.
#
# DATABASE_URL_POOLED="postgresql+asyncpg://azmoth:PASSWORD@ep-cool-darkness-a1b2c3d4-pooler.eu-central-1.aws.neon.tech/azmoth?sslmode=require&channel_binding=require"

# ── Region, and why it is not Azure ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Neon project must be in `aws-eu-central-1` — AWS Europe, Frankfurt.
#
# Not an Azure region, even though the VM is in germanywestcentral and matching them would keep the
# sub-processor list to one name: Neon deprecated all of its Azure regions on 7 April 2026 and no
# longer accepts new projects in any of them, on any plan. `azure-gwc` is gone.
#
# aws-eu-central-1 is still Frankfurt and still the EU, so docs/AVV_TECHNICAL_ANNEX_DRAFT.md § 5.1
# ("ausschliesslich innerhalb der Europäischen Union") holds. What it changes is § 5.2: AWS,
# Neon/Databricks and Grafana Labs (US) all join the sub-processor list. The region cannot be
# changed after the project is created.

# ── The container registry ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing is built on the VM. The images come from GHCR, built by
# .github/workflows/release-images.yml. scripts/deploy.sh writes ENGINE_IMAGE, WEB_IMAGE,
# WEB_BUILDER_IMAGE and IMAGE_TAG into /opt/azmoth/shared/release.env on every deploy — they are
# per-release values, not settings, and are documented here only so that this file answers
# "what does the azure stack read".
#
# GHCR_TOKEN is a GitHub CLASSIC personal access token whose only scope is `read:packages`. It
# cannot push an image, read the repository source, or act on the account:
#   https://github.com/settings/tokens/new?scopes=read:packages
#
# GHCR_USER=your-github-login
# GHCR_TOKEN=ghp_...
# ENGINE_IMAGE=ghcr.io/azmoth-org/azmoth-engine
# WEB_IMAGE=ghcr.io/azmoth-org/azmoth-web
# WEB_BUILDER_IMAGE=ghcr.io/azmoth-org/azmoth-web-builder
# IMAGE_TAG=737a889...

# ── The hostnames Caddy serves ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TWO, and that is the whole list. Substituted into infra/docker/Caddyfile when Caddy loads it;
# unset means Caddy refuses to start rather than serving an unnamed site.
#
# There is deliberately no MARKETING_DOMAIN_HOST. azmoth.com and www.azmoth.com are served by
# Vercel, and this box must not serve them or request certificates for them — see the header of
# infra/docker/Caddyfile. Pointing their A records at the VM takes the public site down.
APP_DOMAIN_HOST=app.azmoth.com
API_DOMAIN_HOST=api.azmoth.com

# Where Let's Encrypt sends warnings when renewal has been failing. A mailbox somebody reads — it
# is the only warning before a certificate lapses.
ACME_EMAIL=ops@azmoth.com

# ── Backups, for infra/scripts/backup-to-azure.sh ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The storage account created by infra/azure/provision.sh, and the age PUBLIC key the dump is
# encrypted to.
#
# The matching private key must NEVER be on the VM — that is the property that makes a compromised
# host unable to read back a single backup. Keep it in a password manager.
#   age-keygen -o azmoth-backup.key      # run this on your laptop
#
# Kept even though Neon has native point-in-time restore, because Neon's Free-plan history window
# is six hours (a rollback, not a backup) and because every Neon mechanism lives inside the Neon
# project — none of them survives a deleted project or a lapsed card. See docs/deploy/AZURE.md § 6.
# STORAGE_ACCOUNT=azmothbackupxxxx
# BACKUP_CONTAINER=db-backups
# AGE_RECIPIENT=age1ql3z7hjy54pw3hyww5ayyfg7zqgvc7w3j2elw8zmrj2kg5sfn9aqmcac8p


# ══ THE LOCAL STACK ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Everything below is read by docker-compose.yml and docker-compose.dev.yml on a laptop. The Azure
# override profiles out the services these configure, so none of it reaches the VM.

# ── Ports on the host ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only the published side. Every container listens on its own conventional port internally.
# Unpublished entirely on the VM by docker-compose.azure.yml — a published Docker port bypasses the
# host firewall, so the NSG alone would not close them.
WEB_PORT=3000
MARKETING_PORT=3001
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
ADMINER_PORT=8080

# ── The local database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The compose defaults are azmoth/azmoth/azmoth and are DEVELOPMENT credentials for the `postgres`
# container. They have no meaning on the VM, which has no such container — there the database is
# Neon and the credentials are inside DATABASE_URL.
POSTGRES_USER=azmoth
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me
POSTGRES_DB=azmoth

# Overrides the URL the engine and Better Auth are handed in the LOCAL stack. Leave unset to have
# it composed from the three values above.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://azmoth:change-me@postgres:5432/azmoth

# ── Public addresses baked into the marketing build ───────────────────────────────────────────
# LOCAL ONLY now. The marketing site is deployed by Vercel, which reads these from its own project
# environment rather than from this file; they are still here because docker-compose.yml can build
# and run the marketing container on a laptop.
#
# Where the product application is reachable BY A VISITOR'S BROWSER. Every "Anmelden" and
# "Kostenlos testen" button on the marketing site resolves against it. It must be a public URL —
# never http://web:3000, which resolves only inside the compose network.
#
# IMPORTANT: a BUILD ARGUMENT, not a container variable. Every page of the marketing site is
# statically prerendered, so this value is baked into the HTML when the image is built; setting it
# on a running container does nothing. Changing it means:
#
#     docker compose -f infra/docker/docker-compose.yml build marketing
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL is accepted as an alias; APP_URL wins when both are set, and is preferred
# because it never reaches the client bundle.
APP_URL=https://app.azmoth.com

# Canonical public URL of the marketing site itself — canonical links, sitemap.xml and og:url are
# built from it. Also a build argument, for the same reason.
MARKETING_SITE_URL=https://www.azmoth.com


# ══ BOTH SHAPES ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Authentication (apps/web) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED in production, and deliberately without a default anywhere. It signs every session
# cookie, so a value that changes between boots signs sessions the next container cannot verify —
# which presents as "everyone is randomly logged out". Generate with:
#
#     openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# Only needed when a proxy terminates TLS on a different hostname than the container sees.
# BETTER_AUTH_URL=https://app.azmoth.com

# ** WHO MAY CREATE AN ACCOUNT. ** Required in production, and the only thing between /signup and
# the open internet — there is no invitation flow and no email verification behind it.
#
# Comma- or whitespace-separated. An entry starting with `@` is a DOMAIN and admits everyone at it;
# anything else is one exact address. Matching is case-insensitive and trims whitespace, so a list
# pasted out of an email works. See apps/web/lib/auth-allowlist.ts.
#
# EMPTY ADMITS NOBODY — that is the intended failure, not a bug. A gate whose unset state is "open"
# is open on every deployment where somebody forgot the variable, and it fails silently. Someone
# has to state who is allowed in. `scripts/deploy.sh --signup-allowlist` writes this value, and
# `scripts/preflight.sh` fails the deployment if it is still empty.
#
# Prefer exact addresses. `@gmail.com` here would readmit the whole internet.
SIGNUP_ALLOWLIST="pilot@praxis-beispiel.de, admin@azmoth.com"

# OPTIONAL, and off unless BOTH are set: with neither, the Google provider is not registered and no
# button is rendered. Google must be told the exact callback under "Authorised redirect URIs":
# https://app.azmoth.com/api/auth/callback/google
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=


# ── Engine ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
APP_ENV=production
DEBUG=false
UNVERIFIED_RULE_POLICY=warn
BASE_FACTOR_POLICY=schwellenwert
SOLVER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=5
CACHE_ENABLED=true

# How long the migration step waits for the database to accept a login before giving up.
#
# It matters more against Neon than it did against a local container. Neon's compute suspends after
# five minutes of inactivity — on the Free plan that cannot be turned off — so the first connection
# of a deploy is a cold start rather than a live server. 60s is generous for a resume that takes a
# second or two, and the cost of it being too short is a failed deploy that reads like a broken
# migration.
MIGRATION_WAIT_SECONDS=60

# How hard the PADnext framing check bites, before the engine reads a single position out of a
# delivery. Both compose files default it to `strict`, so setting it here is an explicit act.
#
#   strict   The application default. A delivery whose framing is wrong is refused — 422,
#            PADNEXT_SCHEMA_VIOLATION, every violation named with a line and a column. Framing
#            means: is this an ADL payload at all — root, namespace, message type, the
#            anzahl/posanzahl counters, behandlungsart, and whether an Abrechnungsfall has
#            positions.
#
#   warn     **THE PILOT SETTING — for real-world data ingestion.** Every violation becomes a
#            finding, the delivery is audited anyway, and the report carries a top-level
#            `schema_warnings` array plus `schema_policy: "warn"`, so nobody can read the result as
#            having come from a conforming file. Each affected delivery also emits one structured
#            log line (`event: padnext_schema_violation`) naming every violation's rule, line,
#            column and element path, under the request id of the upload that carried it.
#
#   off      The schema is not consulted. Not for the pilot: it produces a report indistinguishable
#            from one computed on a conforming file, which is exactly what `warn` avoids.
#
# Why `warn` for the pilot. Our XSD is a hand-written subset of PADneXt v2.12, not the official
# schema, and it has never met a real PVS export. The day-one failure is not a wrong audit — it is
# a real, slightly non-conforming export refused at the door, so the engine never gets to audit it
# at all. `warn` turns that from a 422 the partner cannot act on into a finding we can read, count,
# and correct the subset schema against.
#
# It changes NOTHING about how positions are judged: a goziffer with no @ziffer, an unreadable
# faktor, an unmodelled position type are always findings and never refusals, under every policy.
# It does not relax PADNEXT_ALLOW_REAL_DATA either, which stays false in both compose files.
#
# Set this back to `strict` when the pilot ends, or once the subset schema has been corrected
# against the deviations the pilot logged.
PADNEXT_SCHEMA_POLICY=warn


# ── Analytics (marketing) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LOCAL ONLY, like the rest of the marketing build arguments — on Vercel these live in the Vercel
# project's environment.
#
# Cookieless reach measurement, and off unless all three are set — which is the right default
# until there is something to measure. Nothing is loaded, nothing is stored on the visitor's
# device, and no consent banner appears.
#
# Google Analytics is deliberately not supported: the marketing site argues for the product on
# DSGVO grounds to a German audience, and several supervisory authorities have found GA use
# unlawful over the US transfer question. Plausible and Umami are cookieless, so § 25 TDDDG does
# not bite and no banner is required.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ values, so all three are baked in at build time like APP_URL — changing one means
# rebuilding the marketing image, not restarting the container.
#
#   provider = plausible | umami
#   src      = the script URL (self-hosted or the vendor's cloud)
#   site id  = Plausible: the site domain. Umami: the website id.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ANALYTICS_PROVIDER=
NEXT_PUBLIC_ANALYTICS_SRC=
NEXT_PUBLIC_ANALYTICS_SITE_ID=
